  "prompt": "Write a function named crop_array that takes a multi-dimensional array and a set of slice intervals, and returns a new array containing only the elements defined by those intervals. The function accepts the following parameters: a multi-dimensional numeric array (array) whose shape is defined by a list of dimension sizes, and a list of interval tuples (intervals) where each tuple contains a begin index (integer or null) and an end index (integer or null) corresponding to each dimension of the array. For each dimension, the begin and end values define a half-open slice [begin, end) applied to that dimension. A null begin value indicates the slice starts from the beginning of that dimension. A null end value indicates the slice extends to the end of that dimension. Negative index values are supported and follow standard array slicing semantics (i.e., they index from the end of the dimension). The function returns a new array containing only the elements within the specified intervals. If the length of intervals does not match the number of dimensions in the array, the function should raise a ValueError. If any begin index is greater than or equal to its corresponding end index (after resolving null values), the function should raise a ValueError. If the array parameter is null, the function should raise a TypeError.",
  "implementation_details": "Language: Python. Use NumPy for array creation and slicing. Represent the input array as a numpy.ndarray. Apply slicing using a tuple of slice objects constructed from the intervals list, where each slice is slice(begin, end). Null values in the intervals should be passed directly as None into the slice constructor, which NumPy interprets as the dimension boundary. Return the sliced result as a new numpy.ndarray. Do not use random number generation inside the function itself \u2014 randomness belongs only in test harnesses that generate inputs for this function.",
